# =============================================================================
# intervallum — Latin range type word in an en file (expect parse error).
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • Locale surfaces are sealed — the en spelling of the range type is `range`, so the Latin `intervallum` is refused in an en file
#   • Parse error — `PARSE040:wrong_locale_type` names the word written and the en spelling to use instead
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • writing `intervallum<int>` in an en file — write `range<int>`
#
# See also: intervallum
# =============================================================================

# Latin range type word in an en file (expect parse error)
#
# WHY: en and la spellings never mix in one file; the en pack owns `range`.
#
# DELIBERATE: this exemplum keeps the Latin spelling (`intervallum`) on purpose,
# to pin the PARSE040 `wrong_locale_type` diagnostic for the range type.

main {
    let intervallum<int> r ← 1 ‥ 3
    print r
}
